WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:04.537 line:0%
-The purpose of the watch is
to determine exactly how

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:08.141 line:0%
Anna O'Donnell has survived with
no food.

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:10.377 line:0%
-So you want us to watch her?

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:12.645 line:0%
-Yes.

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:19.552 line:0%
On the 14th day, you will each
present your separate testimony.

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:21.087 line:0%
-How long exactly has it been

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:22.856 line:0%
since the last time
the girl ate?

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:27.093
-Four months.

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:32.331
-That's impossible.

00:00:32.331 --> 00:00:33.699
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:35.368
We're here with Florence Pugh,
everybody.

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:36.402
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:38.604
That was a clip
from "The Wonder."

00:00:38.604 --> 00:00:42.808
Now, this is a period piece, and
I think sometimes when you hear

00:00:42.808 --> 00:00:44.543
that an actor is
doing a period piece,

00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:46.145
they might be drawn to
the costumes

00:00:46.145 --> 00:00:47.914
and the beautiful grandeur
of it.

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:50.449
This is one of the muddiest
period pieces ever.

00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:52.919
-Not glamorous at all.
-No, it's not very glamorous.

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:55.254
Did you enjoy sort of stomping
through the mud?

00:00:55.254 --> 00:00:57.623
-Yes, completely opposite to Amy
in "Little Women."

00:00:57.623 --> 00:01:00.093
-It really is, yeah.
-I had one outfit.

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:01.394
[ Laughter ]

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:02.795
-It was kind of
a one-outfit movie.

00:01:02.795 --> 00:01:05.031
-It is.

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:07.032
-There's a great many animals
on set.

00:01:07.032 --> 00:01:09.936
Did you have any high jinks?
-We shot --

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:12.071
-I should note, this is not
a high-jinks movie.

00:01:12.071 --> 00:01:13.339
-No.
-It's very serious.

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:14.841
-It's very serious.

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:19.378
And thankfully we didn't all
become really serious people.

00:01:19.378 --> 00:01:22.314
But we were shooting on the top
of mountains for months

00:01:22.314 --> 00:01:24.083
and weeks, and it was so windy,

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:27.253
and you'd see all four seasons
in one day.

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.956
And whenever you'd work with
the animals on the mountains,

00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:32.125
they were also like,
"Why are we on a mountain?"

00:01:32.125 --> 00:01:34.660
[ Laughter ]
"It's really windy and cold."

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.664
And there was one take we were
walking with a horse,

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:40.900
and the horse just turned around
sideways and was like, "Nope."

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.536
[ Laughter ]
"Not walking anymore."

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:47.106
And I had a sheep run into me,
and I face-planted in mud.

00:01:47.106 --> 00:01:50.576
Like, full-on mud, and the whole
set had, you know,

00:01:50.576 --> 00:01:52.445
when we were working outside,
we'd shooting in these

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:56.014
tiny little villages that people
would be coming and visiting,

00:01:56.014 --> 00:01:58.218
and, like, fans would would be
coming and waiting, just,

00:01:58.218 --> 00:01:59.919
"It's exciting,
there's a film set."

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:03.356
And on this one day there was
probably 50 to 60 people waiting

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:04.624
on the side of the barriers,

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:06.493
just like with their iPhones
like this.

00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:09.528
And I had a very simple scene
where I had to walk across.

00:02:09.528 --> 00:02:11.930
Just walk across the road
like that. Just walk.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:15.201
But with really intense facial
expressions going on.

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:18.037
And as I was walking past
this sheep,

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:20.406
the sheep wouldn't move
out of the way.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:23.176
So I, like, gently patted
the bum.

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:25.377
And that sheep went like, "Rrr!"

00:02:25.377 --> 00:02:28.314
And then another sheep next to
it went, like, "Rrr!"

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.816
[ Laughter ]
And then that sheep ran into me.

00:02:30.816 --> 00:02:35.788
And I flipped over the sheep and
went straight down into cowpat.

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.657
It was like full-on mud.
It was great.

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:39.325
-And this is when all your fans
were there.

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:41.860
-Yeah, yeah,
filming the whole thing.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.096
There was a photographer there.

00:02:44.096 --> 00:02:45.564
Instantly the next day,
it was on "Daily Mail,

00:02:45.564 --> 00:02:48.101
like, "sheep got better of Flo."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:50.102
Thanks for that.

00:02:50.102 --> 00:02:52.771
-Obviously, you're
an accomplished actor.

00:02:52.771 --> 00:02:54.240
I'm sure this doesn't
bother you.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.309
But is it strange when you're
doing a period piece like that

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:58.077
and you can also see, like,
everybody with --

00:02:58.077 --> 00:02:59.512
-Yeah.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.580
-Fans with modern iPhones
and stuff?

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.217
-Yeah, it's also that --
It's cool.

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:08.087
They're there all day, and so
you kind of have to also be

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:12.191
very aware that it accidentally
is a tough day for them as well

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:15.127
because they've been there
since 6:00 a.m.

00:03:15.127 --> 00:03:17.997
But no, it's --
I love doing those films.

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.732
I love getting in the costume.

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:21.868
I don't like having wet feet,
though.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:24.804
-Yeah. No, there's nothing worth
having wet feet for.

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:27.907
I don't even like going to the
beach because your feet get wet.

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:31.177
-All the shoes are essentially
like a leather sock.

00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:33.946
When you walk around in mud,
your foot is trench foot.

00:03:33.946 --> 00:03:36.382
-It's not great.
-No.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:39.385
-This is, like we were talking
about, a very serious film.

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:42.054
There's superstition.
There's religion.

00:03:42.054 --> 00:03:44.991
I would imagine a fairly heavy
head space to be in

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:46.059
when you're doing a movie
like that.

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:47.460
You did say
you didn't get serious.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.862
But what do you do when you're
on a set like that

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:53.298
to also let yourself have
relaxing thoughts?

00:03:53.298 --> 00:03:57.503
-Right, well, so that -- there
was a secret to that, which was

00:03:57.503 --> 00:03:59.639
when we'd be on a mountain and
it was really, really cold

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:01.975
and really windy and everybody
was like,

00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:04.110
"How are we still here,
oh, my God,"

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.645
me and my mate, my assistant,

00:04:06.645 --> 00:04:09.915
came up to me and Jesse would
look at me like this,

00:04:09.915 --> 00:04:11.851
and I would respond,

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:14.220
"It's a 'Real Housewives of
Beverly Hills' kind of night."

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.056
And so, like, I'd be in this
mountain with all of

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:19.158
these things, and we would
basically be plotting

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.260
when we were going to get home
and watch "Real Housewives."

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.396
[ Laughter ]
And then we'd get home and have

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:26.599
our G&amp;T with our little crackers
and be like...

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:27.900
"What are you going to say?"

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.735
-I will say, watching the film,

00:04:29.735 --> 00:04:32.371
if you said guess what TV show
I was watching while I made it,

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:33.439
that would have been
my last guess.

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:35.474
-I was channeling
"Beverly Hills."

00:04:35.474 --> 00:04:38.378
[ Laughter ]
-Hey, thank you for being here.

00:04:38.378 --> 00:04:40.146
Have a wonderful Thanksgiving.
-Thank you.

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:41.413
-And as always,
a delight to see you.

00:04:41.413 --> 00:04:43.482
You guys, that's Florence Pugh.

00:04:43.482 --> 00:04:45.451
"The Wonder" is in select
theaters now

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:47.619
and available on Netflix
November 16th.

00:04:47.619 --> 00:04:49.422
We'll be right back
with Steve Kornacki.

